  "title": "Surd Sanctuary",
  "description": "Rationalise denominators and tame the wild surds of the algebra forest.",
  "concepts": [
    {
      "term": "Surd basics",
      "def": "A surd is an irrational root like √2 or √3 that can't be simplified to a whole number."
    },
    {
      "term": "Simplifying surds",
      "def": "√12 = √(4·3) = 2√3. Pull out perfect square factors."
    },
    {
      "term": "Adding surds",
      "def": "Only like surds combine: 3√2 + 5√2 = 8√2."
    },
    {
      "term": "Multiplying surds",
      "def": "√a · √b = √(ab), so √3·√12 = √36 = 6."
    },
    {
      "term": "Rationalising",
      "def": "Multiply top and bottom by the surd to clear it: 1/√2 = √2/2."
    },
    {
      "term": "Conjugates",
      "def": "For 1/(a+√b), multiply by (a-√b)/(a-√b) to rationalise."
    }
  ],
